<Layout
	title="About"
	description="Founder-operator moving into programme delivery and digital for development. Building infrastructure for smallholder supply chains."
	pubDate={new Date('Apr 25 2026')}
>
	<p>
		I'm Nikhil Pradhan. I build operational infrastructure for places spreadsheets
		forgot.
	</p>
	<p>
		Since 2021 I've been CEO of <strong>ApiCare</strong>, an agri-tech venture
		operating in Sikkim — India's only fully organic state. We work with 125+
		smallholder beekeepers and farmers across three Himalayan districts. The
		Government of Sikkim selected ApiCare as a private-sector partner under RAMP —
		a World Bank-financed MSME programme the state implements. Our supply chain
		has cleared three KPMG audit cycles with zero findings and we are building
		toward an EU export pipeline.
	</p>
	<p>
		In 2025 I joined the Erasmus Mundus MBA program in Impact Entrepreneurship
		(EMMIE) — a fellowship that's taken me from Zagreb to Vilnius and, in
		September 2026, on to Liège. I graduate in December 2026.
	</p>
	<p>
		Alongside the MBA, I'm building <strong>FarmLedger</strong> — an AI traceability
		and credit-profile platform for the world's smallest farms. It records every
		trade a farmer makes with customers, businesses and FPOs, and multimodal AI
		turns that transaction history into a credit profile a bank can underwrite
		against. The work sits at the intersection of
		traceability infrastructure, smallholder finance, and the kind of supply-chain
		transparency that EUDR will require by 2027. FarmLedger is also the subject of my
		MBA thesis.
	</p>
	<h2>What's next</h2>
	<p>
		I'm moving toward programme management and digital-for-development roles. At
		ApiCare I've been on the delivery end of a public-private partnership —
		government counterparts, audit cycles, training schedules, and
		farmers who need the system to work on the first try. I want to help design
		and run those programmes from the institution side. Graduating December 2026,
		open to relocating anywhere, and open to conversations now.
	</p>
